  "context": [
    "The Compass launched in India in 2017 as Jeep's first locally manufactured model, built at the Ranjangaon plant near Pune on the small-wide 4x4 architecture it shares internationally with the Renegade. A comprehensive 2021 facelift brought a redesigned dashboard, a bigger touchscreen, and sharper exterior styling, while leaving the core mechanicals largely untouched.",
    "Powertrains have stayed consistent through that facelift: a 1.4-litre turbo-petrol offered only in front-wheel drive with a manual or dual-clutch automatic, and a 2.0-litre Multijet diesel offered in both front-wheel drive and a part-time all-wheel-drive setup paired with a 9-speed torque-converter automatic on the range-topping Model S and the AWD trims. The diesel AWD combination has historically been the one genuine off-road buyers hold onto longest.",
    "Resale-wise, the Compass sits behind Korean and Japanese midsize SUV rivals mainly because of Jeep's thinner dealer and service footprint outside large metros, higher-than-average spare-parts costs, and persistent buyer perception that European-platform SUVs are costlier to maintain once out of warranty. That perception shows up directly in the city-level variance below, where Compass resale is weakest in cities with sparse Jeep service coverage.",
    "Jeep's own aggressive end-of-quarter and end-of-model-year discounting on new Compass units has, over the years, compressed the price gap between a brand-new car and a lightly used one, which pulls down the percentage of ex-showroom price a used Compass can command relative to segment leaders like the Creta or Seltos that see thinner new-car discounts."
  ],
  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is the resale value of a Jeep Compass in India?",
      "a": "A well-maintained Compass retains approximately 63% of its ex-showroom price after two years and around 39% after five years, as of September 2026. For a 2022 diesel Night Eagle with 25,000–50,000 km, the current fair-market band is roughly ₹16.08–18.51 lakh."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does the Compass depreciate faster than Korean or Japanese midsize SUVs?",
      "a": "Yes. The midsize SUV segment averages around 58% retention at three years and 44% at five years, while the Compass sits lower at 53% and 39% respectively, reflecting higher perceived running costs and a thinner service network."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does diesel or petrol hold value better on the Compass?",
      "a": "Diesel variants trade higher in absolute terms given their higher ex-showroom price and AWD availability on top trims, but both fuels follow broadly the same depreciation curve — the gap between them stays roughly proportional across every year and km band in current data."
    },
    {
      "q": "How much does a Jeep Compass depreciate per year?",
      "a": "The Compass loses about 24% of its value in year one and a further 13 percentage points by year two, settling into roughly 5–6 percentage points of retention lost per year through year five, when it sits near 39% of ex-showroom."
    },
    {
      "q": "Which city gives the best resale price for a Jeep Compass?",
      "a": "Mumbai and Pune currently trade around 2% above the national mid, supported by an established dealer and enthusiast base. Kolkata trades roughly 3% below baseline due to a sparse service network and higher perceived ownership risk."
    }
  ],
